# ***THE OBJECT LIST IS DERIVED FROM UPSTREAM'S OWN CONFIGURE, NOT
# TRANSCRIBED.***  php's `configure' was run on the build host with the
# flags this port wants and `PHP_GLOBAL_OBJS' plus `PHP_CLI_OBJS' read
# out of the generated `Makefile' -- the clisp precedent, where the
# 86-name bootstrap list came from `makemake.in's own shell.  The flags
# were:
#
#	--disable-all --enable-cli --disable-cgi --disable-phpdbg
#	--without-pcre-jit --disable-opcache-jit --disable-fiber-asm
#	--disable-zend-signals --disable-huge-code-pages --without-pear
#
# **`--disable-all' does not mean a small build in 8.5**: 513 objects,
# of which `ext/lexbor' is 186.  `ext/date', `ext/hash', `ext/json',
# `ext/pcre', `ext/random', `ext/reflection', `ext/spl',
# `ext/standard', `ext/uri' and `ext/opcache' are all unconditional --
# there is no `--disable-opcache' at all, only `--disable-opcache-jit',
# which is what drops the 13-file `jit/ir' code generator.
#
# ***THE OBJECTS KEEP THEIR PATHS AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC.***  Sixteen
# basenames collide across the 43 source directories -- `array.c' is
# both `ext/standard/array.c' and `ext/lexbor/lexbor/core/array.c', and
# `hash', `parser', `state', `token', `url' and ten more are the same
# -- so a flat list would silently build one and link it twice.  php is
# a PROGRAM rather than a library, so there is no archive to collide
# in and nested object paths cost nothing but the `mkdir' in the rule
# below.
#
# The comments inside this list each end with a backslash, which is
# `mkcont-sweep.py's rule: a comment that ENDS a continuation ends the
# variable, and the rest of the list would vanish without a word.

